Task 3: Web Image Optimization &amp; React Integration
High-resolution AI outputs typically range from 2MB to 8MB in PNG format. To maintain mobile app
performance and meet Core Web Vitals targets (&lt;200KB target weight), images must be re-encoded
into WebP format.
Option A: Automated Build Script using Sharp (npm)
Below is a Node.js script using the `sharp` library to dynamically resize and encode images into
optimized WebP format.

// optimize-image.js
const sharp = require(&#39;sharp&#39;);
const path = require(&#39;path&#39;);
const inputPath = path.join(__dirname, &#39;src/assets/food_scooter_raw.png&#39;);
const outputPath = path.join(__dirname, &#39;public/assets/food_scooter.webp&#39;);
sharp(inputPath)
.resize({ width: 800 }) // Resize width to 800px, auto-scale height
.webp({ quality: 80, effort: 6 }) // Convert to WebP with 80% quality compression
.toFile(outputPath)
.then(info =&gt; {
console.log(`Optimization Complete! Saved to ${outputPath}`);
console.log(`Final File Size: ${(info.size / 1024).toFixed(2)} KB`);
})
.catch(err =&gt; console.error(&#39;Error optimizing image:&#39;, err));
Option B: Online Tools Alternative
For non-scripted workflows, use TinyPNG.com, Squoosh.app, or EZGIF to compress PNG assets,
set width to 800px, and export as `.webp` (achieving ~85% reduction from ~3.2MB to ~145KB).
